Stage 4 pins the theorem to the submitted program: a mechanical normalized-constructor comparison found the closure’s name, one parameter, body, and defining environment identical to trusted regeneration, with only the parser’s explicit empty-list terminators normalized. Fresh ground instances of the membership, loop, and entry preconditions all proved; `[2,1,2]` yields `[1,2]` in the K claim, candidate Python, and canonical witness.

Stage 5 found no candidate-local unsound rule. The exhaustive inventory contains 1,029 entries (248 syntax declarations, 775 rules, five contexts, one configuration), including all 15 proof-local entries. The two priority bridges have bridge-free universal claims over their full arbitrary-continuation match domains; the four pure summaries are total, disjoint/exhaustive, and structurally descending. The substantive limitation is in the supplied model, not the candidate: symbolic membership uses structural equality, while concrete execution/CPython collapse `True`, `1`, and `1.0`; symbolic sorting is also the supplied opaque `sortVS` primitive. Both boundaries are explicit in the candidate’s trust ledger.
Stages 4 and 5 passed the soundness/pinning gate. Mechanical body comparison and fresh ground witnesses pin the exact source; the exhaustive 1,029-entry inventory found the 15 proof-local declarations/rules sound. Supplied-model numeric identification and opaque sorting remain trust-boundary concerns.
